review_text:
Beautiful tactical.

Pros :

- kill stuff (well, mostly guards) in a beautiful Edo-era Japan ;
- various array of tactics to take down your enemies, with challenging situations (you probably won't be bored with that one);
- each character has a great personality, which adds even more flavor to a nice storyline ;
- to be a ninja or a kunoichi really feels good in this game.

Cons :

- you will scratch your head more than once with this one. Prepare to die a lot, and retry a lot to fully handle what you have to do ;
- if you want to unlock everything, arm yourself with patience, for you will retry stuff over and over and over....

Time to scarper... this samurai is getting suspicious of me.

1) What kinda ninja carries only a single shuriken on him? The answer is Mimimi's Hayato because they railroad you;

2) Some walls can be walked on; some cannot, despite that said wall is only about 40 centimetres below the one you are standing on at the moment. A similar example is those so-called grappling hooks that can only grapple onto predetermined “anchors”. Why? Because Mimimi railroads you;

3) During the heat of a siege, despite deafening explosions and the exchange of gunfire everywhere between the two armies, the extremely sensitive ears of your enemies can still pick up the throes of death from a single comrade; then they abandon their firing positions and rush straight to the scene to seek you out. During said all-out war, the disappearance of a single foot soldier is so extraordinary, raising so much alarm that entire squads abandon their firing positions to come after you. (since no one is supposed to die during a freaking war!) During the same war, when only the castle itself remains standing with its riflemen on the parapets taken out, not a single soldier from the opposing camp thinks of maybe invading the castle and instead, they keep firing from behind cover at phantoms. Why? Because it’s too much trouble to work out a new set of mechanisms so let’s just butcher realism and common sense instead!

4) Enemies can tell at a glance from afar whether certain footprints in the snow belong to a friend or foe??? Or maybe friends leave no footprints? Ghosts? Elves?

5) When you eliminate a bloodthirsty warmonger and pre-emptively stop a genocide of thousands from happening, you fail the mission; why? Because you’ve had the guts to leave the sacred rail tracks!

6) The sadistically tight positioning of enemies in later stages reduces the game to a frustrating chore;

[CONCLUSION] Get the game for nostalgia’s sake if you want and sing its praises, because with nostalgia’s pink tinted glasses anything can be forgiven.

That’s it; now I need to go get my worn out F5 and F8 keys replaced.
